WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.700
C# and ASP.NET Core applications commonly use ILogger and Microsoft.Extensions.Logging as the base logging model.

2
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.280
The value of that model is consistency.

3
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:26.780
Controllers, minimal APIs, services, background workers, middleware, and infrastructure code can all emit events through a familiar abstraction while different providers send those events to console output, files, centralized systems, or cloud services.

4
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:35.140
Log categories, providers, sinks, log levels, scopes, and structured properties all shape usefulness.

5
00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:40.040
Categories help readers understand which component emitted the event.

6
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.140
Providers and sinks decide where the event goes.

7
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.680
Log levels communicate severity and expectedness.

8
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:56.260
Scopes carry shared context such as ExampleRequestId, ExampleTenantId, or ExampleCorrelationId across a set of related events.

9
00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:02.140
Structured logging is usually safer and easier to query than unreviewed string concatenation.

10
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:09.280
Instead of creating one long message with mixed values, teams can record a stable message template and named properties.

11
00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:14.500
This makes searching, filtering, dashboards, and alerting more reliable.

12
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.960
It also makes it easier to decide which properties are allowed and which should be redacted or omitted.

13
00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.160
Consistent event names and message templates help long-term maintenance.

14
00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:28.900
An event such as ExampleEventName should mean the same thing wherever it appears.

15
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:36.520
If every team invents different labels for the same situation, monitoring becomes noisy and incident review becomes slower.

16
00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:40.140
Naming discipline is a security and operations habit.

17
00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:43.220
Logging configuration should vary by environment.

18
00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.920
Development can use more detail to support local troubleshooting.

19
00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:58.540
Production should avoid broad debug-level logging, excessive framework detail, raw request data, and verbose dependency output unless there is a controlled and temporary reason.

20
00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:05.680
Logging configuration belongs in application security review because it controls what information leaves the application boundary.

21
00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:09.480
Centralizing logging conventions reduces drift.

22
00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:17.820
Teams should document approved levels, event categories, property names, redaction rules, and provider settings.

23
00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:24.720
A shared convention lets developers add useful logs without inventing a new privacy and operations model each time.
